Part C: 10 Important Viva Questions and Answers
Q1. What is Quick Sort?
Quick Sort is a sorting algorithm based on the divide-and-conquer technique. It selects a pivot and partitions the array into smaller subarrays.

Q2. What is a pivot?
A pivot is an element selected from the array that is used to divide the array into smaller and larger elements during partitioning.

Q3. What is deterministic Quick Sort?
It is a Quick Sort algorithm that selects its pivot using a fixed method, such as choosing the first or last element.

Q4. What is Randomized Quick Sort?
It is a variation of Quick Sort that selects the pivot randomly to reduce the chances of consistently unbalanced partitions.

Q5. What is the average time complexity of Quick Sort?
O(n log n).

Q6. What is the worst-case time complexity of Quick Sort?
O(n²), when the pivot repeatedly creates highly unbalanced partitions.

Q7. What is the difference between Las Vegas and Monte Carlo algorithms?
Las Vegas: Always produces the correct result, but execution time may vary.
Monte Carlo: Has bounded execution time but may produce an incorrect result with some probability.

Q8. Why is Randomized Quick Sort better than deterministic Quick Sort?
It reduces the dependence on the original input arrangement by selecting pivots randomly, resulting in O(n log n) expected time complexity.

Q9. What is the space complexity of Quick Sort?
Average case: O(log n).
Worst case: O(n).
